## Why This Is Asked
Tests IPC basics, race-free data sharing, and practical use of CAS and memory barriers in a beginner-friendly setting.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free synchronization
- Atomic operations and memory ordering
- Ring buffer wraparound
- Empty/full conditions and visibility

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { atomic_uint head, tail; Message buf[N]; } Channel;

bool send(Channel *c, Message m){
  unsigned long t = atomic_load_explicit(&c->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  unsigned long w = (t + 1) % N;
  if (w == atomic_load_explicit(&c->head, memory_order_acquire)) return false; // full
  while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&c->tail, &t, w, memory_order_acq_rel, memory_order_relaxed));
  c->buf[t] = m;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  return true;
}

bool recv(Channel *c, Message *out){
  unsigned long h;
  do {
    h = atomic_load_explicit(&c->head, memory_order_relaxed);
    if (h == atomic_load_explicit(&c->tail, memory_order_acquire)) return false; // empty
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&c->head, &h, (h+1)%N, memory_order_acq_rel, memory_order_relaxed));
  *out = c->buf[h];
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add a timeout or backoff when the buffer is full/empty?
- How would you adapt this for multiple producers or consumers without locks?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of memory cache eviction with per-process quotas, correctness under concurrency, and interaction with remote backends.

## Key Concepts

- Per-process quotas and accounting
- Per-page state (clean/dirty, eviction-claimed, evicted) and CAS-based races
- Eviction flow: candidate selection, quota enforcement, writeback coordination
- Data structures: per-CPU LRU, global eviction gate, writeback queues
- Remote store latency impact and fairness guarantees

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal eviction sketch
typedef struct page {
  atomic_int ref;
  int owner;                  // pid
  atomic_bool dirty;
  atomic_bool evicted;
  atomic_bool claimed;
} page_t;

bool evict_candidate(page_t *p, int owner_quota) {
  if (atomic_load(&p->dirty)) return false;      // never evict dirty before writeback
  if (atomic_load(&p->claimed)) return false;    // another thread claiming it
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&p->claimed, 0, 1)) {
    // update owner quota here atomically (pseudo)
    atomic_store(&p->evicted, 1);
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test fairness and prevent starvation under bursty eviction pressure?
- How do you handle re-accesses to recently evicted pages from the remote store to avoid thrashing?

