## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of timer mechanisms, wakeup latency, and concrete data structures suitable for embedded or real-time operating systems.

## Key Concepts
- Timers with absolute expiry and optional periodicity
- Heap-based scheduling for efficient expiry checks
- Interrupt-safe wakeups and drift prevention
- Latency testing with mixed workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
function timer_add(heap, expiry, period, task) {
  push(heap, { expiry, period, task });
  siftUp(heap, heap.length - 1);
}

function timer_interrupt(now_ms) {
  while (heap.length > 0 && heap[0].expiry <= now_ms) {
    const timer = popMin(heap);
    timer.task();
    if (timer.period > 0) {
      timer.expiry = now_ms + timer.period;
      push(heap, timer);
    }
  }
}
```

## Testing Considerations
Run mixed CPU-bound and timer-heavy workloads simultaneously. Measure wake latency (actual vs. expected wake time) and jitter (variance in wake intervals) to validate drift prevention and real-time guarantees.

## Why This Is Asked
Signals interrupting kernel I/O are a core but subtle area; beginners must reason about races and correctness.

## Key Concepts
- Blocking I/O, signals, race conditions, atomic flags, wakeups
- EINTR semantics and partial reads
- Memory visibility and CAS-based state updates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-C implementation sketch for read_with_signal
int read_with_signal(int fd, void *buf, int n, int *interrupted){
  int total = 0;
  while (total < n){
    int got = kernel_read(fd, (char*)buf + total, n - total);
    if (got > 0){ total += got; continue; }
    if (pending_signal(fd_thread_id)){ *interrupted = 1; return -1; }
    // block until wake
  }
  return total;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle multiple signals, and nesting of signals?
- How would you test race conditions between signal delivery and I/O completion?
